From: Riccardo Manfrin <3090891+riccardomanfrin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:37:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix keepalive pool block (#20)

* device: add non-blocking pool accessors

TryGet, and the Device-level wrappers around it, report failure instead of
waiting on the pool's condition variable. Nothing calls them yet: this is a
pure addition so the change of behavior in the keepalive path lands on its own.

* device: never block on the buffer pool while sending a keepalive

SendKeepalive took buffers with the blocking pool accessors. It runs inside a
keepalive timer callback, which holds the timer's runningLock, and Timer.DelSync
waits on that lock. So on a Device whose pool is capped and exhausted -
PreallocatedBuffersPerPool non-zero, plus a peer that stages packets while it
waits for a handshake that never completes - the callback parks in WaitPool.Get
and Peer.Stop can never finish. Peer removal, and therefore Device.Close, are
stuck for the lifetime of the process, and the buffers those operations would
have freed are the ones the callback is waiting for.

Use the non-blocking accessors and skip the keepalive when the pool is dry.

* device: name the handshake-initiation flag after what it does

isRetry only ever decided whether handshakeAttempts is cleared; it said nothing
about the packet or the timers. Rename it to resetAttempts and invert every
call site accordingly. Behavior is unchanged: each caller keeps the same effect
on the counter it had before.

* device: let a peer give up on a handshake it keeps failing

expiredRetransmitHandshake gives up after MaxTimerHandshakes attempts, flushes
the staged queue and stops the keepalive timer. That is the mechanism that frees
the buffers held for a peer which never completes a handshake, and it never ran:
SendStagedPackets asked for a counter reset, so every packet aimed at the down
peer put the attempt count back to zero and, with traffic still arriving, the
limit was never reached.

Stop resetting from the data path. The counter is still cleared by
timersHandshakeComplete, timersStart and the rekey paths, which are the events
that actually mean the peer is reachable.

* device: cover the capped-pool keepalive deadlock

Two regression tests, both failing before the previous two commits:
SendKeepalive on a device whose pool is drained never returns, and
SendStagedPackets resets handshakeAttempts so the give-up-and-flush path is
never reached. Plus unit coverage for TryGet at capacity and on an uncapped
pool.

* device: reproduce the deadlock on a live device pair

A functional test next to the unit ones: two devices with a completed
handshake, a third peer that can never handshake, real traffic and real timers.

The shape matters and the first attempt at it did not reproduce anything. A
peer whose staged queue is full never allocates for a keepalive - SendKeepalive
returns early - so the peer that drains the pool is not the peer that gets
stuck. The victim is one with a live session and an empty staged queue: its
keepalive does allocate, parks in the exhausted pool holding the timer's
runningLock, and removing that peer then waits on DelSync forever.

Without the keepalive fix this fails with "removing a peer never completed"
after the 10s guard; with it the removal returns and the test takes ~2.5s. The
cap is lifted in a cleanup so a failing run can still close the devices instead
of hanging the package.
